    "text_delta": "otes and anonymized scoring sheets (e.g., via HR records or a Subject Access / Freedom of Information request).\n\n#### C. Evaluate the feedback objectively\nWhen (or if) you receive feedback, assess:\n* Did they benchmark you directly against the published job specification?\n* Are the reasons specific and evidence-based (e.g., specific budget sizes, union negotiations, or turnaround-time metrics)?\n* Or are the explanations vague and subjective (e.g., \"better fit,\" \"more commanding presence\")?\n\n#### D. Consult trusted advisors before raising a formal issue\nIf you decide to raise concerns about the panel's gender composition or procedural fairness:\n* Speak confidentially with a professional union representative, staff association, or an external mentor.\n* Document everything: keep copies of the job description, your submitted application, interview dates, panel members, and all email correspondence.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe all-male panel is a **legitimate point of concern and a potential indicator of procedural weakness or affinity bias**, but bringing it up directly as an accusation early on may cause the institution to become defensive. \n\nYour strongest immediate move is to insist on structured, competency-based feedback. If the hospital fails to provide defensible scoring or if you find that panel composition violated internal equity policies, you will have much firmer ground on which to challenge the process.\n",
